      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1362,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance that is essential for the survival of most known forms of life. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless liquid.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, from a chemical and physical perspective:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\nWater is a compound made up of two elements:\n*   **Hydrogen** (2 atoms)\n*   **Oxygen** (1 atom)\n\nIts chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. This means that for every single molecule of water, there are two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\n### 2. Molecular Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means the molecule has a slight electrical charge imbalance.\n*   Oxygen is more \"electronegative\" (it pulls electrons toward itself) than hydrogen.\n*   This creates a \"dipole,\" where the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge.\n\nThis polarity is what makes water unique. It allows water molecules to stick together (a property called **cohesion**) and to stick to other substances (a property called **adhesion**).\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nBecause of its structure, water has several properties that are vital for life and the Earth's climate:\n\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water is excellent at dissolving other substances. It can carry nutrients and minerals into cells and carry waste products out. This is why it is often called the \"universal solvent.\"\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature and keeps the human body from overheating.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n*   **Expansion upon Freezing:** Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze solid from the bottom up, killing all aquatic life.\n\n### 4. States of Matter\nWater can exist in three different states, which are all part of the **water cycle**:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (crystalline structure).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we use for drinking, bathing, and oceans.\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible gas in the air).\n\n### 5. Biological Importance\nWater is the main component of the human body (about 60%). It is used for:\n*   Transporting nutrients and oxygen to cells.\n*   Removing waste (urine, sweat).\n*   Lubricating joints.\n*   Regulating body temperature through sweating and panting."
